--TEST--
Core\Jwt::signObject declares `object` claims and a key pair, so a scalar, an array and a shared secret are each refused where the call is written rather than thrown from the body — `rule:security/protocol-roster`, `rule:core-api/shape-rules`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The refusals this member does not have to write. `object` is satisfied by a
// class instance and by a shape and by nothing else, so the member never sees a
// payload that is not a JSON object and never owes a sentence about one. The
// key is the same: a structured payload is a token issued to another party, and
// the only key such a party can check is the public half of a pair.
secret bytes $shared = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
Core\Crypto\KeyPair $pair = Core\Crypto::generateKeyPair(Core\Crypto\KeyKind::P256);

Core\Jwt::signObject("sub=ada", 15m, $pair);
Core\Jwt::signObject(["sub" => "ada"], 15m, $pair);
Core\Jwt::signObject(Core\Arr::shapeAs<{sub: string}>(["sub" => "ada"]), 15m, $shared);
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `object`, found `string`
   --> case.nvs:10:22
   |
10 | Core\Jwt::signObject("sub=ada", 15m, $pair);
   |                      ^^^^^^^^^ this is `string`

error[E0401]: expected `object`, found `array<mixed>`
   --> case.nvs:11:22
   |
11 | Core\Jwt::signObject(["sub" => "ada"], 15m, $pair);
   |                      ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ this is `array<mixed>`

error[E0401]: expected `Core\Crypto\KeyPair`, found `secret bytes`
   --> case.nvs:12:79
   |
12 | Core\Jwt::signObject(Core\Arr::shapeAs<{sub: string}>(["sub" => "ada"]), 15m, $shared);
   |                                                                               ^^^^^^^ this is `secret bytes`

error: aborting due to 3 errors
